<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sotho people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Southern Sotho, Basotho, Basuto</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Basotho are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> people of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> who speak Sesotho, living mainly in Lesotho and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s Free State and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauteng">Gauteng</a> provinces. The Basotho nation was formed in the 1820s and 1830s by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe I</a>, who gathered refugees from the wars of the period around his mountain stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu">Thaba Bosiu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sotho-people.jpg" alt="Sotho people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lesotho.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Basotho are a Sotho-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tswana-people">Tswana people</a>, a mixture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> clans that mixed with the San who already lived in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Their ancestors are believed to have come from north-east Africa and to have migrated south in the fifth century CE. Separation from the Batswana is assumed to have taken place by the 14th century, and by the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-working">iron-working</a> was well established in Basotho communities.</p><p>By at least the 17th century a series of Basotho kingdoms covered the southern part of the African plateau, in what is now the Free State, parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauteng">Gauteng</a> and the North West. Society was highly decentralised: each kraal, or extended clan, was ruled by its own chief, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> were united only in loose confederations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 1820s refugees from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> expansion under Shaka came into contact with the Basotho of the highveld. In 1823 one group, the Kololo, migrated north and in 1845 conquered Barotseland. Moshoeshoe I meanwhile unified a loose confederacy of Basotho clans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> peoples, and shaped refugee groups escaping the Difaqane into a cohesive nation.</p><p>He established his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butha-buthe">Butha-Buthe</a> in 1822 and later moved it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu">Thaba Bosiu</a>. To meet the advance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> voortrekkers he encouraged French missionaries of the Paris Evangelical Missionary Society, who advised him on foreign affairs, helped him to buy weapons and, between 1837 and 1855, set down Sesotho orthography and printed its first books.</p><p>After losing the western lowlands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-state-basotho-wars">Free State–Basotho Wars</a>, Moshoeshoe appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> in 1868 to make Basutoland a British protectorate, with a British administration at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maseru">Maseru</a>. Borders were drawn in 1869, leaving large numbers of Sesotho speakers in the Orange Free State. Moshoeshoe died in 1870 and was buried on the summit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu">Thaba Bosiu</a>. Basutoland became independent in 1966 as the Kingdom of Lesotho.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Sesotho is the first language of about 1.5 million people in Lesotho and of almost 4 million in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> according to the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, where it is one of eleven official languages. Missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> opened the way to a written literature, including Thomas Mofolo's epic Chaka. Because of the central part missionaries played in securing Moshoeshoe's kingdom, most Basotho became Christian, often blending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> with older beliefs in Modimo, a supreme being approached through the ancestors. Lesotho men have long migrated to South African cities to work in the mines. Traditional dress includes the mokorotlo, a conical hat, and the Basotho blanket, worn over the shoulders or waist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sotho people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Silozi, the language of the Lozi kingdom, is mainly derived from <b>Sotho</b>, the speech of the Kololo who ruled the floodplain for only about 25 years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cape fought the Gun War of 1880–1881 when it tried to disarm the <b>Basotho</b>, a costly failure that led it to return Basutoland to direct British rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Orange Free State took shape on the high grasslands between the Orange and Vaal rivers, where Voortrekkers and other Dutch-speaking farmers from the Cape Colony settled from the 1830s among Griqua, Barolong, <b>Sotho</b> and other communities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>There he built a new kingdom by absorbing <b>Sotho</b>- and Nguni-speaking chiefdoms already living in the area, the emakhandzambili, and by incorporating refugees from the wars of the period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swazi Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Wars with the Orange Free State in 1858 and 1865-1866 cost the <b>Basotho</b> much of their lowland farmland.</blockquote><a href="/state/basotho-kingdom">Basotho Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Cape attempts to disarm the <b>Basotho</b> caused the Gun War of 1880-1881, which the Basotho effectively won, and in 1884 Britain resumed direct rule as a High Commission territory from Maseru.</blockquote><a href="/state/basutoland">Basutoland</a></li></ul></section>
